      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm working on a low-fat mayo formulation and having issues with phase separation in the high-acid stages. can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface when the pH is low?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin is a natural emulsifier that contains phospholipids, which have both hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions. In a low-acid environment, when the mayonnaise formulation faces phase separation, these phospholipids align at the oil-water interface with their hydrophilic ends towards water and hydrophobic ends towards oil. This creates a barrier that prevents the oil droplets from coalescing, thus maintaining a stable emulsion. In general, by reducing surface tension at the oil-water interface, lecithin helps to keep the mixture homogenous, even under high-acid conditions. Adjusting the concentration of lecithin or incorporating specific additional stabilizers may help enhance stability in your low-fat mayonnaise formulation as well.",
