    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'm also seeing some weird behavior where small clusters of oil seem to \"break away\" from the main emulsion during the high-speed shear process, even with the lecithin present. is there a specific kinetic mechanism where the acid interferes with the phospholipid bilayer's ability to stay anchored at the interface?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During high-speed shear processing, the emulsifying action and stability of lecithin can indeed be challenged, particularly in acid environments, which could affect protein conformation and the PV cap distribution around the oil droplets. It seems that the kinetic energy of the mixing process might be disrupting the delicate balance of forces that lecithin acts to maintain. The low pH can affect the charge of the phospholipid heads and proteins, diminishing their ability to effectively anchor at the interface, hence causing \"break away\" clusters. Adjusting the stirring speed, temperature, or pH to optimize the conditions that favor stabilization—beyond just passing the lecithin alone—may mitigate this issue. You may want to explore the addition of a buffering system to maintain pH or the use of alternative emulsifiers that are more shear-resistant, alongside processes that carefully control the shear history of your mayonnaise.",
      "source": "target",
