    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Upon nearing the isoelectric point of the proteins within your emulsion, the lecithin's efficiency in stabilizing the emulsified droplets can be compromised. As acidity increases, phospholipids can become protonated, reducing the electrostatic repulsion between them and leading to reduced charges (lowering the negative surface charge) on the crests of the phospholipid aggregates. Concurrently, the proteins may unfold or aggregate, exposing hydrophobic domains which can further precipitate the destabilization of the emulsion. These changes in the phospholipid and protein disposition might diminish the interfacial film's ability to prevent coalescence of the oil droplets aggressively. Alternative strategies might involve increasing the concentration of negatively charged emulsifiers or introducing more resilient proteins that maintain their charge and structure under acidic conditions. These enzymatic or kinetic responses highlight a dynamic and evolving state under shear, necessitating a delicate equilibrium between all components, which can be influenced heavily by pH and processing conditions.",
